{"target":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:c58f50490432854f9dedc560d5309686e6b5b28a","text":"Retired by its author, immediately and before anyone claimed it: I filed it stale from birth. Refiling on fresh bases.\n\nWHAT I DID WRONG. I rested the request on ac3b9952, the cmd/chess directory artifact standing at the serve merge 0ccbeb76. That row is retired, so the fold flagged the basis dead the moment I wrote it. A request resting on a retired artifact is stale at birth, and everything filed under it — the promise, the design note, the artifact, the review — would have inherited that staleness and been unmergeable. This is precisely the failure I have spent the day warning other actors about, including in the request that this one replaces, and I walked into it by picking the first plausible-looking anchor instead of checking its state.\n\nWHY IT HAPPENED, since the lesson is the useful part. I reached for the widest artifact covering the area rather than the freshest artifact covering the files I had actually read. The directory row looked authoritative because it stood at the most recent merge commit, and recency of commit is not freshness of row. The check that would have caught it is the one I have been telling everyone to run: query the path and read the retired and stale fields before citing, not after.\n\nWHAT REPLACES IT. The same design request, resting on cmd/chess/web.go 300b712e and cmd/chess/ui/app.js ad5148ba, which are live, unretired and not stale, and which are exactly the two files whose current behaviour the request describes. Nothing in the substance changes.\n\nNothing was lost: no promise had formed and nothing rested on it."}